        <header class="head">
            <h1>James Hetfield</h1>
        </header>
        <img class="imagens" src="<?= BASE_URL ?>/media/james.jpg">
        <p class="paragrafo"><b>Full name:</b> James Alan Hetfield<br><b>Instrument:</b> Vocal, Guitar<br><b>Born
                on:</b> August 3, 1963<br><b>Hometown:</b> Los Angeles, California<br><b>Characteristics:</b> 1.85 m,
            81.65 Kg, brown hair, blue eyes.<br><b>Before Metallica:</b> Played in Obsession, Leather Charm.<br><b>Pre-Metallica
                career:</b> Worked in a sticker factory.<br><b>Listens to:</b> Black Sabbath, Motorhead, Skynyrd, Thin
            Lizzy, Tom Waits, Nick Cave, Spaghetti Western Soundtracks<br><b>Movies:</b> The Good, The Bad and The Ugly,
            Easy Rider, Sling Blade, Pulp Fiction, Vanishing Point<br><b>Heroes:</b> Clint Eastwood, Pops
            Hetfield.<br><b>First Concert:</b> Aerosmith & AC/DC, 1978, Long Beach, California.<br><b>Considers
                himself:</b> Rude, honest, stubborn.<br><br>James Hetfield is a rarity in the world of rock 'n' roll. A
            frontman who preferably stays in the background when discussing his band. The most secretive member of
            Metallica, James makes no secret of the fact that he dislikes doing interviews and prefers to let his music
            speak for him. On rare occasions when he does speak, he is honest, objective, and extremely humble, all
            characteristics that apply to Metallica as a band. Personally, he can be shy or uninhibited, quiet or loud,
            depending on his mood. Ask six different people who have known him what he likes, and you'll get six
            different answers.<br><br>The son of strict Christian Scientists, James was a quiet child, into sports -
            "not a Dennis the Menace". His older brother played drums in a local band and turned James on to rock in
            general, and Black Sabbath in particular. In third grade, the Hetfield family moved from Downey to LaBrea,
            California, where James attended a real school. "It was the kind of place where it was taboo to like sports
            and have long hair." James kept his hair and innocence within his guitar. He played while "in the school
            band" Obsession, mostly doing covers of Zeppelin and Sabbath, and later formed Leather Charm.<br><br>It was
            around this time that James met Ron McGovney, who would soon be the first bassist of Metallica. It wasn't
            until James met Lars Ulrich, however, through an ad in the Recycler newspaper, that Metallica was formed.
            Originally the band's singer, James did very well playing rhythm guitar while they couldn't find someone
            else with the right touch. Later, Metallica was looking for a frontman, so James (who humbly describes
            himself as a "singer or yeller or screamer") managed to concentrate on guitar and singing at the same time.
            He believed he could get distracted doing both things at once. But that never happened and he continues to
            do both. Metallica simply wouldn't be Metallica any other way.<br><br><br>
        <header class="head">
            <h1>Lars Ulrich</h1>
        </header>
        <img class="imagens" src="<?= BASE_URL ?>/media/lars.jpg">
        <p class="paragrafo"><b>Full name:</b> Lars Ulrich<br><b>Instrument:</b> Drums<br><b>Born on:</b> December 26,
            1963<br><b>Hometown:</b> Gentofte, Denmark.<br><b>Characteristics:</b> 1.70 m, 58.97 Kg, light brown hair,
            green eyes.<br><b>Before Metallica:</b> None.<br><b>Pre-Metallica career:</b> Newspaper delivery boy, gas
            station attendant.<br><b>Listens to:</b> Oasis, AIC, Black Grape, his wife.<br><b>Heroes:</b> Guilhermo
            Vilas (Argentine tennis player), Ritchie Blackmore, Ace Frehley.<br><b>First Concert:</b> Deep Purple, 1973,
            Copenhagen.<br><b>Considers himself:</b> Hasty, stubborn, spontaneous, sarcastic, communicative, jealous,
            tattletale.<br><br>One of Metallica's co-founders, Lars is often called to be the band's spokesperson.
            Talkative, often intensely serious, especially when discussing his band.<br><br>Born and raised in
            Copenhagen, young Lars followed in his father's footsteps as a tennis player, competing seriously in the
            sport until his family moved to Los Angeles in 1980. Shortly after realizing he was only crawling near the
            good tennis players, Lars abandoned tennis as a career and pursued his great vocation and passion: music,
            especially drums. Skilled on the drums and hitting cardboard boxes with sticks since the age of 12, Lars
            convinced his grandmother to buy a drum kit in 1977 and practiced at home imagining he was Ian Paice from
            Deep Purple.<br><br>Once in Los Angeles, Lars was disheartened by how difficult it was to find someone who
            had been influenced by Iron Maiden, Saxon, Motorhead, and other European metal bands. He looked for a good
            guitarist who was willing to form a heavy metal band, and he found James Hetfield, a guy who appreciated the
            best things in heavy metal. A few months later, they made the birth of Metallica in Lars' garage. We doubt
            that the kid who dreamed of playing at Wimbledon regretted changing his career to the one he has now,
            playing in stadiums of a different kind than he had imagined before, when he was a tennis player.</p><br>
        <header class="head">
            <h1>Kirk Hammet</h1>
        </header>
        <img class="imagens" src="<?= BASE_URL ?>/media/kirk.jpg">
        <p class="paragrafo"><b>Full name:</b> Kirk Lee Stuart Hammett<br><b>Instrument:</b> Guitar<br><b>Born on:</b>
            November 18, 1962<br><b>Hometown:</b> San Francisco, California<br><b>Characteristics:</b> 1.70 m, 60.78 Kg,
            brown hair and eyes.<br><b>Before Metallica:</b> Played in Exodus.<br><b>Listens to:</b> Jimi Hendrix, UFO,
            Black Sabbath, Deep Purple, Led Zeppelin, Kiss, John Coltrane, Goul, Mule, Chet Baker, Prez Prado, AIC,
            David Bowie, U2, King Crimson, Robert Johnson.<br><b>Heroes:</b> Boris Karloff, Bela Lugosi, Jimi Hendrix,
            Chet Baker, Diane Arbus, John Coltrane, Charles Bukowski, Edward Munch, Joel Peter Whitkin.<br><b>First
                Concert:</b> Day on the Green with Eagles, Steve Miller, Heart, and Foreigner.<br><b>Considers
                himself:</b> Scented, ugly, happy, obsessive, progressive, curious.<br><br>Kirk Hammett has the
            distinction of being the most consistent and content member of Metallica. Usually in a good mood and
            infallibly genial, his sunny nature may belie the fact that he is a serious, very talented
            guitarist.<br><br>Near the end of the 60s, Kirk's older "hippie" brother turned him on to Jimi Hendrix,
            Santana, Led Zeppelin, and other sounds of the time. When his brother Rick was away at school, young Kirk
            would pick up his brother's guitar and try to play. He started playing seriously when he was 15 and formed
            his first garage band "about 2 weeks later".<br><br>Kirk, a friend who had recently acquired a bass, and
            another friend who sang, plugged everything into a single amplifier in the bassist's garage. Their first
            sets consisted of 2 songs: "Wild Thing" and "Purple Haze" by Hendrix, including "15 minutes of horrible
            guitar solos". Conveniently, the amplifier (borrowed from Kirk's school) exploded.<br><br>After school, Kirk
            went to work at a grocery store and a diner to afford guitars and an amplifier. At 20, he decided to take
            guitar lessons and studied with Joe Satriani for about a year to perfect his technique. In 1981, Kirk and
            drummer Tom Hunting formed the band Exodus. They performed in local clubs and once played a gig with
            Metallica, certainly before the band flew to New York to record their first album. This was while Metallica
            was deciding that guitarist Dave Mustaine had to leave the band. When Kirk received an invitation to join
            the band, he thought it was an April Fool's joke. It wasn't a joke; Kirk flew to New York where he met
            James, Cliff, and Lars. Kirk recalls that it was a strange situation because he didn't really know them. He
            only realized he was hired when he was included in the recording of the album "Kill 'Em All". Casually, the
            beginning of what would be a long-standing relationship. Kirk's creativity and cool-headedness are a quarter
            of what makes Metallica great.</p><br>
        <header class="head"><h1>Robert Trujillo</h1></header>
        <img class="imagens" src="<?= BASE_URL ?>/media/trujillo.jpg">
        <p class="paragrafo"><b>Full name:</b> Roberto Agustín Miguel Santiago Samuel Trujillo Veracruz Batista<br><b>Instrument:</b>
            Bass<br><b>Born on:</b> October 23, 1964<br><b>Hometown:</b> Santa Monica,
            California<br><b>Characteristics:</b> 1.75 m, 82 Kg, brown eyes and dark brown hair.<br><b>Before
                Metallica:</b> Played in Suicidal Tendencies, Infectious Grooves, Jerry Cantrell, Ozzy Osbourne.<br><b>Listens
                to:</b> Black Sabbath, Larry Graham, Bootsy Collins, Marcus Miller, John McLaughlin, Jimmy Johnson,
            classical music, rock, funk, and jazz.<br><b>Influences:</b> Jaco Pastorius, Geezer Butler, Larry
            Graham.<br><b>Considers himself:</b> Stubborn, easygoing, quiet, loud, steady, troubled.<br><br>Bassist
            Robert Trujillo (Roberto Agustin Miguel Santiago Samuel Trujillo Veracruz; Santa Monica, California, October
            23, 1964) played with Suicidal Tendencies, Black Label Society, Infectious Grooves, Jerry Cantrell, Ozzy
            Osbourne, and joined Metallica in 2003.<br><br>Trujillo's career began when he met Mike Muir and Rocky
            George from the band Suicidal Tendencies. He showed good command of the bass and played with the group for a
            considerable period. His style and talent were appreciated by the band, which led him to be their bassist.
            In the 90s, Muir and Trujillo started the funk rock band ''Infectious Grooves''. Robert was the band's
            bassist for several years until he left the band and joined Ozzy Osbourne. He remained there until 2003,
            when he was invited to join the great and famous American thrash metal band, Metallica. The band wasn't
            unknown to Trujillo. He had met them before when his band opened for Metallica at a show in 1994 during the
            Summer Shad Tour.<br><br>To record Metallica's St. Anger album, Robert was offered $1,000,000. This price
            was, according to Lars Ulrich, because they wanted to show how serious they were. Between Jason Newsted's
            departure and Robert Trujillo's arrival, Bob Rock (former producer from 1991-2006) was the band's temporary
            bassist.
            <br><br><br>
